schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Is a DAO Right for Your Community?
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: comparison
  summary: >-
    A plain-language decision guide for permaculture/grassroots communities
    weighing DAO structure, using a community-garden analogy to explain
    decentralized/autonomous/organization, listing when a DAO fits (everyone
    wants a voice, global participation, transparency, rewarding
    contribution) and when it doesn't (fast emergency decisions, need for
    legal protection, low tech comfort, governance-attack risk), a five-
    question discussion guide, and cooperative/sociocracy/simple-voting-tool
    alternatives.
  audience: permaculture and grassroots community organizers evaluating governance structures
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      radical-transparency-and-global-participation-vs-no-legal-protection-and-real-technical-learning-curve
  related_concepts:
    - dao-alternatives-cooperatives-sociocracy-lightweight-voting-tools
  related_resources:
    - loomio
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/is-dao-right.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: >-
    assessed-but-excluded: a generic "DAO" concept-lineage object was not
    created here to avoid duplicating what the still-open what-is-dao.md
    work order is expected to define directly; this ingestion instead
    captures the article's distinctive angle (governance-attack risk and
    non-DAO alternatives) as claim-evidence and concept-lineage objects.
